/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - The diffusivity DAB is the proportionality constant in Fick's first law for A diffusing through B.
   - The orders matter in design.
   - Two-bulb method for gases.
   - Δy₀ is the initial mole-fraction difference; Δy is the difference at time t; S is the tube cross-section in m²; L is the tube length in m; V is each bulb volume in m³; t is time in s.
   - A plot of ln(Δy₀/Δy) against t is a straight line through the origin, and DAB comes from its slope.
   - ρL is the liquid density (kg/m³), M its molar mass (kg/mol), c = P/RT the gas concentration (mol/m³), (yB)lm the log-mean air fraction between the surface and the top.
   - For liquids the diaphragm cell is standard.
   - Kinetic theory gives the Chapman–Enskog equation, right to a few per cent for non-polar gases.
   - DAB in cm²/s, T in K, P in atm, M in g/mol, σAB in Å.
   - DAB in cm²/s; T in K; P in atm; M in g/mol; ΣvA and ΣvB are sums of tabulated diffusion-volume increments (not molar volumes).
*/
