/* book Worked example 2: Q: Estimate the diffusivity of ethanol (A) at low concentration in water (B) at 298 K by Wilke–Chang. Water: μB = 0.89 cP, MB = 18.02, φ = 2.6. Le Bas volumes: C 14.8, H 3.7, O (in an alcohol) 7.4 cm³/mol. The measured value is about 1.24×10⁻⁵ cm²/s.
   1. Molar volume of C₂H₅OH: VA = 2(14.8) + 6(3.7) + 7.4 = 59.2 cm³/mol, so VA0.6 = 11.57.
   2. (φ MB)1/2 = (2.6 × 18.02)1/2 = 6.84.
   3. DAB = 7.4×10⁻⁸ × 6.84 × 298 / (0.89 × 11.57) = 1.47×10⁻⁵ cm²/s = 1.47×10⁻⁹ m²/s.
   4. Compare: (1.47 − 1.24)/1.24 = 0.18, so the estimate is 18 % high, inside the usual 10 to 20 % band for this correlation.
   Answer: DAB ≈ 1.47×10⁻⁹ m²/s, about 18 % above the measured 1.24×10⁻⁹ m²/s and four orders of magnitude below a gas diffusivity. */
